  <h2>Why Eye of Round Is the Best Meat for Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Eye of round — a cylindrical muscle from the rear leg — wins for three reasons:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Near-zero fat:</strong> Less than 3% fat means jerky stays fresh longer. Fat is the primary cause of rancidity in stored jerky.</li>
    <li><strong>Uniform shape:</strong> The cylindrical muscle produces consistently sized slices from end to end — no awkward thin edges or thick centers.</li>
    <li><strong>Firm texture:</strong> The dense muscle fibers hold up to slicing thin without tearing and produce a satisfyingly chewy finished jerky.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>London Broil for Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>London broil is a cooking method, not a cut — but the label is almost always applied to top round in grocery stores, making it an excellent choice for beef jerky. Buy the thickest London broil you can find and slice it across the grain into 1/4-inch strips. The large, flat muscle slices beautifully.</p>

  <h2>Flank Steak Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Flank steak produces jerky with a more pronounced texture due to its visible grain structure. Cut with the grain for stringy, pull-apart jerky, or against the grain for a cleaner bite. Slightly higher fat than round cuts means better flavor but a shorter shelf life — consume within 1 week at room temperature.</p>

  <h2>Brisket Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Brisket jerky is richer and more intensely beefy than round cuts. Use the flat cut (leaner) for longer shelf life or the point cut (fattier) for maximum flavor. Brisket jerky is especially popular in smoker recipes — the connective tissue and fat render beautifully with low-and-slow smoke, producing intensely savory results.</p>

