  <h2>How to Cut Meat for Beef Jerky</h2>
  <h3>Against the Grain (Recommended)</h3>
  <p>Cutting <strong>perpendicular to the muscle fibers</strong> shortens the fibers in each slice. This produces jerky that breaks apart cleanly with each bite — the standard chewy-but-not-stringy texture most people prefer.</p>

  <h3>With the Grain</h3>
  <p>Cutting <strong>parallel to the muscle fibers</strong> keeps fibers long and intact. This produces stringy, pull-apart jerky — more work to chew, but some people prefer this old-school texture, especially for thick-sliced jerky.</p>

  <h3>How Thick to Cut Beef Jerky</h3>
  <p>The standard is <strong>1/4 inch (6mm)</strong>. Thinner than 1/8 inch produces crunchy, crumbly jerky. Thicker than 3/8 inch takes significantly longer to dry and risks an uneven moisture gradient. If you want tender jerky, go slightly thicker (3/8 inch) with the grain. For crispy jerky, go thinner (1/8 inch) against the grain.</p>

  <img src="/images/eye-of-round-roast.webp" width="1200" height="896" alt="Whole eye of round beef roast on dark butcher paper — the best cut for homemade jerky" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;" loading="lazy"  decoding="async"/>

  <h2>How Much Beef Makes 1 lb of Jerky?</h2>
  <p>Beef loses roughly 50–60% of its weight during dehydration as water evaporates. Plan on <strong>2–2.5 lbs of raw beef</strong> to produce approximately 1 lb of finished jerky. A 2 lb batch fills most home dehydrators and produces about 3–4 cups of jerky pieces.</p>
